Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

87
00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.120
We're The Office Ladies.

88
00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:45.530
Hi, you guys.

89
00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.130
Welcome to Diwali.

90
00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:50.990
I'm so excited for this episode, Jenna.

91
00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:51.930
Me too.

92
00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.050
It's a great episode, and we have a great special gueSt.

93
00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:56.730
I just want to get to it.

94
00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:57.570
All right.

95
00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.850
Well, this is Season 3, Episode 6.

96
00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:05.590
It was directed by Miguel Arteta and written by our special guest, Mindy Kaling.

97
00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:06.350
Woo-hoo!

98
00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:07.990
Very, very exciting.

99
00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.250
Well, let's get to it.

100
00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:10.830
We are going to waste no time.

101
00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.230
I'm starting with a summary.

102
00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:12.870
Do it.

103
00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:19.590
In this episode, everyone from Dunder Mifflin attends a traditional Indian celebration of Diwali in honor of Kelly.

104
00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:27.710
Now, Ryan is going to meet Kelly's parents, who are really less than impressed with his commitment to their daughter.

105
00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:33.590
Well, to be fair, he says with extra money, he wants to travel and get like an Xbox or something.

106
00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:34.430
So, you know.

107
00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:34.790
Yes.

108
00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.370
I think I would also be less than impressed.

109
00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:42.370
Meanwhile, Michael proposes to his girlfriend, Carol.

110
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:43.370
Oh, Michael.

111
00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:43.710
Wow.

112
00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:44.550
Oh, Michael.

113
00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.250
Michael takes a big step.

114
00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.510
In front of a lot of strangers.

115
00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:50.350
Yeah.

116
00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:50.930
So awkward.

117
00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:52.210
It's a bit of a crash and burn.

118
00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:53.010
So awkward.

119
00:04:53.390 --> 00:05:00.970
Pam enjoys herself for the first time as a single person, and Angela guards the shoes while eating some dry naan.

120
00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.570
Very dry naan.

121
00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:10.270
And over at the Stanford office, Jim, Andy, and Karen work late into the night and booze it up.

122
00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:11.110
Yeah.

123
00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:12.490
Jägermeister.

124
00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.750
I mean, what the fuck?

125
00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:17.650
Andy is a maniac.

126
00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.890
I'm going to give you fast fact number one.

127
00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:30.130
This is a really special episode because The Office was the first American television comedy series to depict the Hindu festival of Diwali.

128
00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:31.110
I love that.

129
00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:32.410
Way to go, The Office.

130
00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:40.010
It aired November 2nd, 2006, and it was seen by 8.8 million people on the night that it first aired.

131
00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.150
And now it's been seen by how many people?

132
00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:43.730
I don't even know.

133
00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.570
Did you know what Diwali was, Jenna, when we got the script?

134
00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:51.230
Shelby B asked the same question, and I did not know about Diwali.

135
00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:52.830
Did you know about Diwali?

136
00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:54.930
I did, but I grew up overseas, you know.

137
00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:56.030
I grew up in Indonesia.

138
00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.590
Bali has a very big Hindu population.

139
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:05.390
So I had heard of Diwali, and I thought it was awesome that we were going to do an episode about it.

140
00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:07.210
Had you ever celebrated Diwali?

141
00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.990
I had never celebrated it, but myself, I went to an international school.

142
00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:16.070
They did recognize, you know, many holidays, and there was like a Diwali day.

143
00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.030
Yeah, well, they do that at my kid's school now.

144
00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:25.190
They have a day where they celebrate Diwali, which I think is really cool, especially since I grew up not knowing about it.

145
00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.210
It is so beautiful, and it was so fun.

146
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:28.910
I remember we were all really giddy.

147
00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.070
Of course, this meant we got to leave the office.

148
00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.790
That brings me to fast fact number two, Angela.

149
00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.110
Fan question from Juliet G and Phoebe Tomac.

150
00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:41.270
Where did you shoot the scenes when you were celebrating Diwali?

151
00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:43.890
Well, guess who I went to for the answer?

152
00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.830
Does it rhyme with Schmentish Media?

153
00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:48.110
It does, and we have a sting for it.

154
00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:48.670
You do?

155
00:06:54.970 --> 00:07:00.430
Yes, Angela, that was sent in by Tyler Beckwith, and we love it, Tyler.

156
00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.270
Thank you for our Kentopedia sting.

157
00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:03.050
It is really great.

158
00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:06.690
It sounds like something out of like a 1980s action kind of movie.

159
00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:14.410
Well, Kentopedia told us that Diwali was shot at Grant High School.

160
00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:23.110
We shot most of the scenes in their gym, which is called Grant Hall, and we shot there at the end of August, and school was in session.

161
00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:25.490
They had just started up the school year.

162
00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:35.810
He said that we shot there for three full days, and we had to work around the school schedule because, like, school was happening in the hallways.

163
00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:44.930
I have such a fond memory of our time shooting there because they parked our trailers facing sort of like a little field with some trees,

164
00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:49.710
and normally they were just in an asphalt parking lot.

165
00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:58.150
And so I would open my door, and there'd be trees and grass, and Brian brought a football, and we, like, tossed the football out in the grass.

166
00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:00.570
And I have such fond memories of this whole week.

167
00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.390
I remember it, too, for those exact same reasons.

168
00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:11.850
Well, this school is host to a lot of filmmaking, including Freaks and Geeks, Black-ish, Malcolm in the Middle, and True Blood.

169
00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:14.090
And I've got a little bit of trivia for you.

170
00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:14.810
Yeah?

171
00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.330
Melora Harden went to Grant High School.

172
00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:18.250
Get out of town.

173
00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.530
Yeah, we shot at her alma mater.

174
00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:21.170
Wow.

175
00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:22.770
I love that.

176
00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:25.910
She's, like, a true sort of, like, Hollywood kid, you know?

177
00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.950
Like, there's all these things from her life, like, right here.

178
00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:33.790
To me, it's still all sort of, like, I still feel like a transplant in some ways in Los Angeles.

179
00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:41.110
Like, still to this day, if I'm ever out and about and I see the Hollywood sign, I'm like, there's a Hollywood sign.

180
00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.610
Oh, my God, it's a Hollywood sign.

181
00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:52.590
But if I drive by and I see, like, a film crew filming something on the street, I get as excited as if they pulled up in my neighborhood in St. Louis as a kid.

182
00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:53.850
I'm like, ooh, what are they filming?

183
00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:55.650
Oh, look, I wonder what it is.

184
00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.550
I wonder if we can find out who's the star.

185
00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:58.330
Yeah, I look.

186
00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.310
Like, I get that same reaction still.

187
00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:00.650
Yeah, me, too.

188
00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:03.150
I drive by and I try to see, like, is there someone famous out there?

189
00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:04.670
Exactly.

190
00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:06.410
So true.

191
00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:10.930
Well, I'm going to move us along, Angela, because I know we want to get to our interview with Mindy.

192
00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:15.630
Fast fact number three, I said earlier this was directed by Miguel Arteta.

193
00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.750
Angela, I was such a huge fan of Miguel.

194
00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:27.670
When I found out he was going to direct this episode, this is the only episode he directed, I completely geeked out.

195
00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.770
Okay, he had directed this little—

196
00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:31.370
Did you go up to him?

197
00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:32.210
Were you dorky?

198
00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:32.770
What'd you do?

199
00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:36.430
Oh, Angela, I followed him around the entire week.

200
00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:36.990
We bonded.

201
00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:40.910
At the end of this week, I thought we were best friends and would work together forever on every show.

202
00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:52.610
And I'm not going to lie, every time there was, like, a script that I was sent or when I was trying to produce a movie myself, he was the first person I would go to.

203
00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:57.630
Because I was like, I'm literally, like, this guy is a genius.

204
00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:58.510
I love him.

205
00:09:58.730 --> 00:09:59.510
I love that.

206
00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.850
I didn't know you were, like, following him around everywhere.

207
00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:06.410
Oh, yeah, I wanted everything about him to rub off on me.

208
00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:16.190
So he had directed this little indie movie called Chuck and Buck, written by and starring Mike White, who, by the way, I also have stalked.

209
00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.810
Yeah, I know that you stalked him.

210
00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:28.250
I got to work with him, and then that made me feel one step closer to Mike White, who I eventually managed to stalk and become—I became friends with Mike White.

211
00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:32.390
And then I eventually got to be in a Mike White movie starring Ben Stiller.

212
00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:41.430
And then after that happened, I was like, well, I can now retire because I have worked with my idol.

213
00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:45.050
I wrote in my journal about this week—do you want to hear what I wrote, lady?

214
00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:46.370
Of course.

215
00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:47.990
I always want to hear about your journal.

216
00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:49.650
Your journal delights me.

217
00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:52.070
It does.

218
00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:53.890
That's going to be your autobiography.

219
00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.430
A journal delights me by Jenna Fisher.

220
00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.890
I wrote that, and I wrote this.

221
00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:06.410
Me and the adorable Mindy Kaling are going to be doing really early morning interviews together on all the local NBC stations.

222
00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:12.510
We are going to talk to 16 cities, and we have to get up at 5 a.m.

223
00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:13.630
Oh, my gosh.

224
00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:15.550
You guys did a little press junket.

225
00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:17.570
I think this is my very first one.

226
00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:21.930
Well, what's crazy about that, Angela, is you probably then, after you were done, had to come to work.

227
00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:25.970
Oh, yeah, because we had to—I remember we had to be on stage.

228
00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:35.390
We actually did them on the set with the set as the background, and we got there, and we met this satellite media tour is what they called it, and we met their crew,

229
00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:38.150
and we had to be there at 4 a.m.

230
00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:46.510
to go through hair and makeup, and then we started doing interviews at 5 a.m., and we talked to a new city every 15 minutes for three hours.

231
00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:46.970
Wow.

232
00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:47.270
Yeah.

233
00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:49.250
And you did it with Mindy.

234
00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:51.190
Mindy was my first press junket.

235
00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:54.830
Well, this is a wonderful segue into our upcoming segment.

236
00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:59.150
We'll take a break, but when we come back, we're going to play our interview with Mindy Kaling.

237
00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:00.510
She came and talked to us.

238
00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:01.190
It was amazing.

239
00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:07.170
It was such a joy to have her here, and I'm so excited for you guys to listen to this.

240
00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:10.110
And then afterwards, we're going to fully break down the episode.

241
00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.790
We'll get to that right after the break.

242
00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:12.890
We'll see you with Mindy.

243
00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:26.480
Hi, guys.

244
00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:27.020
Hi.

245
00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:28.400
I'm so happy to be here.

246
00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:30.060
Welcome to Office, ladies.

247
00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:32.540
I'm giving you a hug through the Zoomy Zoom.

248
00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:33.260
Thank you.

249
00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:36.680
Congrats, you guys, on the massive success of this.

250
00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:37.960
This is awesome.

251
00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:43.380
Everyone in my life is obviously such a fan, and I'm so proud of you guys.

252
00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:44.120
This is so great.

253
00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:45.120
Thank you.

254
00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:46.180
Thanks, Mindy.

255
00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:47.020
Yeah, of course.

256
00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:47.680
We love it.

257
00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:48.840
We're having a good time.

258
00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:53.780
It's fun to hear the way that the show was through your guys' eyes, because it's so

259
00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:58.300
loving and fun and super accurate, and I'm really enjoying it.

260
00:12:58.440 --> 00:12:59.760
So this is great.

261
00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:00.600
Thanks for having me.

262
00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:01.680
Thank you.

263
00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:02.940
That was so nice.

264
00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.120
I feel like this is all just pure joy.

265
00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:10.360
I'm just so delighted to see you and to talk to you, and this is such a fun episode.

266
00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.280
So we just get to reminisce a little bit.

267
00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:12.860
Love it.

268
00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:17.800
Well, before we get into Diwali, we always like to ask all of our guests their office

269
00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:18.780
origin story.

270
00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:21.880
So how did you come to be on the show?

271
00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:30.100
Well, it was 2004, and I was doing a play off-off-Broadway in New York called Matt and

272
00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:35.500
Ben, which was a play that I wrote with my writing partner and friend at the time, Brenda

273
00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:41.640
Withers, where it's like an hour-long short play where I play Ben Affleck, and she plays

274
00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:42.640
Matt Damon.

275
00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:46.020
It's so hard to tell this story without being like, just being like, okay, so just

276
00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:48.780
ignore that kind of interesting fact and then move on.

277
00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.320
But so we did that.

278
00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:54.880
It won the Fringe Festival, got some attention, and sort of was our entree to LA.

279
00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:00.640
And when we were in LA, I had like an Arrested Development spec and then that play.

280
00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:03.000
And I had very few meetings.

281
00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:09.540
The climate for hiring writers, if you were like an untested Indian American woman, it

282
00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.880
was very different 16 years ago than it is now.

283
00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:17.100
And the only place that I got an interview at, I got two interviews.

284
00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:21.740
One was at Nevermind Nirvana, which was a pilot about an Indian family.

285
00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:24.940
And I was like, yes, I can, yes, I could write on that show.

286
00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:27.460
And the other one was The Office.

287
00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:33.100
And I remember Joey was like, do you guys remember how huge Joey was when we started

288
00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:33.440
shooting?

289
00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:34.000
Yeah.

290
00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:34.480
Oh, yeah.

291
00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.120
The Friends spinoff.

292
00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:37.660
It was like the NBC.

293
00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:38.860
I think they put all their money in that.

294
00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:40.000
And then Father of the Pride.

295
00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:41.060
Do you guys remember that one?

296
00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:41.840
No.

297
00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:48.660
Father of the Pride was a cartoon, a Jeffrey Katzenberg produced cartoon about, from the

298
00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:52.740
point of view of two tigers that worked with Siegfried and Roy.

299
00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:53.600
Oh, yes.

300
00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:54.060
What?

301
00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:54.660
Yes.

302
00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:55.640
It's coming back to me.

303
00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:56.720
And it was an animated show.

304
00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:59.380
This sounds like a bit on Saturday Night Live.

305
00:14:59.560 --> 00:14:59.740
Yes.

306
00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:01.240
This doesn't sound like a TV show.

307
00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.280
Not only was it a real show, it was like the hottest show.

308
00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:05.620
Like, you could not get an interview.

309
00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:07.600
So, I didn't get an interview on Will & Grace.

310
00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:10.480
I didn't get an interview at Joey.

311
00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:13.240
And I didn't get an interview at Father of the Pride.

312
00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:17.340
And you can imagine, like, just to show how things have become so different from them.

313
00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:21.700
Like, can you imagine, like, now, like, a minority woman who won the Fringe Festival

314
00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:22.760
coming out to L.A.

315
00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:26.180
not getting a meeting on, like, on all the two shows.

316
00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:27.260
So, things have changed a lot.

317
00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:27.900
So, that's great.

318
00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:32.880
But the two people who I got interviews with Nevermind Nirvana, and I went into their offices

319
00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:33.580
in Burbank.

320
00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:38.100
This had never happened to me before, but I sat in the office, and as I was there waiting

321
00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:41.460
to meet the showrunner, they got the call that the pilot hadn't been picked up

322
00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:41.880
to series.

323
00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:46.180
So, I sat there, and then the assistant came out, and I was like, you should go home.

324
00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:47.460
Like, there's no jobs.

325
00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.720
And so, I just, like, got up and left.

326
00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:53.980
And then, the second one was at the office with Greg Daniels.

327
00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:55.620
And so, I met with him.

328
00:15:55.720 --> 00:16:01.080
And just so you know, my agent at the time, who I won't mention, was really like, yeah,

329
00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.120
this show doesn't have a lot of heat.

330
00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:06.020
Like, I don't think it's really going to do anything.

331
00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:07.700
Everyone likes the British one better.

332
00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:11.920
So, it's just like, you know, it's good to meet Greg Daniels.

333
00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:13.140
He's, like, done a lot of good stuff.

334
00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.100
But, like, it's just good.

335
00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:15.480
It's just good to, like, have a general.

336
00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.400
So, I went in there, and there was not a lot of stakes.

337
00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:24.620
And I had a very, a really fun interview with Greg that lasted two hours.

338
00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:25.680
But you guys know Greg.

339
00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.000
So, it was actually probably only 15 minutes of talking in two hours.

340
00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.280
And, and then he hired me.

341
00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:35.340
And, or he didn't, he didn't hire me in the meeting.

342
00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:36.920
Like, they still had to do upfronts and everything.

343
00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:39.520
But we had a great meeting, and I heard it went well.

344
00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:43.120
And then, like, a month and a half later, I was told I was coming back for six episodes.

345
00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:44.820
Such a small first season.

346
00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:46.980
But I was so new.

347
00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:50.540
And I think you guys were, you know, new enough, too, that it was just like, oh, my gosh,

348
00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:51.300
any episodes?

349
00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:52.360
That's fantastic.

350
00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:55.880
Not knowing that that was kind of an unusually small number of episodes.

351
00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:58.120
But that was, that was how I got on The Office.

352
00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:02.120
And how did you end up then playing a role on the show?

353
00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:08.320
You know, he had put, Greg had put into the contract, these acting, these writing contract,

354
00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:13.080
these acting clauses for, I think, a number of the writers.

355
00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:14.100
At least it was in mine.

356
00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:15.600
And I think Paul Lieberstein's.

357
00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.820
So, I guess in the back of his mind, he was thinking in those terMs.

358
00:17:18.820 --> 00:17:23.380
But the first season, classic episode, Diversity Day, written by the great B.J.

359
00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:25.340
Novak, I remember

360
00:17:25.340 --> 00:17:30.580
in the room, he loved this idea, Greg loved this idea of Michael Scott offending all these

361
00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:30.960
people.

362
00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:35.860
But it's like, it's kind of the funniest version of it when there's like some minority people

363
00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:37.860
there, too, who can get extra offended.

364
00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:43.220
So, at that time, I think it just made sense to have another minority person.

365
00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:45.380
And I was like, I'll do it.

366
00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:46.540
I actually, I didn't volunteer.

367
00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:49.040
He's like, I had two lines in that episode.

368
00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:50.220
And he said, do you want to be in it?

369
00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:51.020
And I was like, of course.

370
00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:55.620
And so, Diversity Day, yeah, thank heavens that was the second episode.

371
00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:58.320
Because then I guest starred then.

372
00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:02.180
And I think maybe once or twice that first season, I wasn't in it very much.

373
00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:06.160
And then second season was like, I sort of saw, I got more lines.

374
00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:07.820
And yeah, that's how it happened.

375
00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:13.620
What blew me away, Mindy, is when I think back on that, and I was sort of reading back

376
00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:18.440
on Wikipedia about all of us and where we were at in our lives at that time, and you

377
00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:19.280
were 24.

378
00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:22.900
And that blows my mind.

379
00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:25.200
You had your act together.

380
00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:30.160
To me, for someone at 24, you were amazing.

381
00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:34.540
Because at 24, I think I was doing takeout at Chin Chin's.

382
00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:36.180
I wasn't a waiter.

383
00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:38.220
I was just doing a hostess job.

384
00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:39.620
Do you know what it is?

385
00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:40.640
Well, thank you for saying that.

386
00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:43.620
I mean, I love to feel like I was a wonder kid.

387
00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:44.700
But you know what it is?

388
00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:49.180
Is that it's just the life of a writer, a comedy writer, and the life of an actor, they're

389
00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:51.100
just so, they're so different, right?

390
00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:54.060
Like, it's so, it's very hard to break into comedy writing.

391
00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:56.620
But I do think that is easier than breaking into acting.

392
00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:02.640
Because if you can write, you can write your own opportunities and write a spec script that,

393
00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:04.200
you know, then you can use and send out.

394
00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:08.700
Whereas like, when you're a young actor, you're like, I just hope someone calls me in to see

395
00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:09.020
something.

396
00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:10.540
So thank you for saying that.

397
00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:17.020
But you know, it also, it felt less special because BJ's there and he was the same age

398
00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:19.040
and he'd already written on a show at 24.

399
00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:20.440
Oh my gosh.

400
00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:23.600
So it's just when I was like, oh, I guess I'm like young and cool.

401
00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:27.360
He was like, oh yeah, I've already worked on, he was on a Bob Saget sitcom.

402
00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:33.240
And Mike Schur was like 28 and had already had like an Emmy and been in SNL for nine years

403
00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.080
or something crazy.

404
00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:40.600
So there was no sense of like, you could ever think you were special or like ahead of the

405
00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:42.400
curve or anything like that with that group of people.

406
00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:44.200
Well, I always thought you were special.

407
00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:44.780
Thank you.

408
00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:45.680
Thanks, Angela.

409
00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:47.640
All right.

410
00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:49.420
Should we get into this episode, Jenna?

